1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:14,227
Nadia Okafor did not decide to become a whistleblower.
Nobody ever does. She simply noticed

2
00:00:14,247 --> 00:00:20,887
things, one at a time, and wrote them down, and one
night in the winter of twenty twenty-eight

3
00:00:20,907 --> 00:00:26,981
she opened the folder on her phone called Recipes
and realised that it was no longer a

4
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:28,539
folder. It was a case.

5
00:00:30,109 --> 00:00:33,327
The manifest had been the first
thing. There were others.

6
00:00:33,898 --> 00:00:40,115
There was Deshawn at the loading dock, who liked her
because she remembered his daughter's

7
00:00:40,135 --> 00:00:46,006
birthday, and who mentioned, one evening, that the
old building across the river took

8
00:00:46,026 --> 00:00:51,967
a delivery of liquid nitrogen every nine days.
Enough, Nadia calculated in her head as

9
00:00:51,987 --> 00:00:57,997
he said it, to keep about four hundred litres of
material at cryogenic temperature — or

10
00:00:58,017 --> 00:01:03,888
to run a cold room the size of a garage. The old
building's basement had six freezers

11
00:01:03,908 --> 00:01:10,195
from the nineteen-nineties and a vault Pell had built
in the spring. None of it used liquid

12
00:01:10,215 --> 00:01:10,819
nitrogen.

13
00:01:11,388 --> 00:01:17,491
There was the electricity. Umbrella's facilities team
published a sustainability report,

14
00:01:17,511 --> 00:01:23,891
because Catherine had insisted on one, and the report
listed the power use of each building.

15
00:01:23,911 --> 00:01:29,944
The old building on the south bank — four floors, a
basement, a few dozen people — drew

16
00:01:29,964 --> 00:01:33,213
more power than the entire
twelve-storey tower.

17
00:01:33,784 --> 00:01:39,710
And there was Evelyn. Nadia had watched her, twice,
from the car park of the old building

18
00:01:39,730 --> 00:01:45,656
at night, step into the freight elevator in the
basement with a sealed case, and go down.

19
00:01:45,676 --> 00:01:51,469
The freight elevator went to the basement and the
loading dock. It did not go down. And

20
00:01:51,489 --> 00:01:55,544
both times, Evelyn Marsh had not come
back up for nine hours.

21
00:01:57,614 --> 00:02:03,346
None of it was proof. All of it was a smell. And
Nadia Okafor had spent her whole career

22
00:02:03,366 --> 00:02:07,660
learning to trust a smell before she could
see what was making it.

23
00:02:09,729 --> 00:02:15,253
The proof, when it came, came because Evelyn Marsh
had stopped thinking of other people

24
00:02:15,273 --> 00:02:15,890
as a risk.

25
00:02:16,461 --> 00:02:22,387
It was a Thursday in February. Evelyn had come up from
below to sign off the second-generation

26
00:02:22,407 --> 00:02:27,828
assay, which was still, officially, her programme,
and she had been called away in the

27
00:02:27,848 --> 00:02:33,395
middle of it by one of the research associates, who
had appeared in the doorway and said

28
00:02:33,415 --> 00:02:38,772
something too quietly for anyone to hear. She had
gone with him at once. She had left

29
00:02:38,792 --> 00:02:40,163
a binder on the bench.

30
00:02:40,734 --> 00:02:46,608
It was a black binder. It had no title on the spine.
Nadia, walking past on her way to

31
00:02:46,628 --> 00:02:52,572
the lifts, glanced at it, and stopped, and — with
the terrible slowness of a person who

32
00:02:52,592 --> 00:02:57,439
knows she is about to do something that
cannot be undone — turned back.

33
00:02:59,008 --> 00:03:01,564
The first page she saw was a photograph.

34
00:03:02,135 --> 00:03:08,571
It had been taken through thick glass, in cold light.
It showed a cryogenic capsule, very

35
00:03:08,591 --> 00:03:14,809
large, and inside it, in a pale mist, a figure. The
figure was grey, and hairless, and

36
00:03:14,829 --> 00:03:21,047
far too big. Beneath the photograph, in Evelyn's
neat, small handwriting, was a label.

37
00:03:21,067 --> 00:03:24,963
T-zero-zero. Subject. Week
eighty-one. Vitals nominal.

38
00:03:27,033 --> 00:03:33,287
Nadia looked at it for a long time. She told herself it
was a model. A sculpture. A prosthetic

39
00:03:33,307 --> 00:03:39,026
from a film studio. She told herself that for about
four seconds, which was as long as

40
00:03:39,046 --> 00:03:40,694
her training would allow.

41
00:03:41,264 --> 00:03:42,727
Then she turned the page.

42
00:03:43,298 --> 00:03:49,847
It was a table. Twenty-four subjects. Rhesus macaques
— the twenty-four from the Singapore

43
00:03:49,867 --> 00:03:56,125
manifest, she understood at once, with a lurch in
her stomach. Each one had been given

44
00:03:56,145 --> 00:04:02,695
a compound labelled P-strain derivative, in rising
doses. The column on the right recorded

45
00:04:02,715 --> 00:04:03,352
outcomes.

46
00:04:03,923 --> 00:04:05,765
Nineteen of them said deceased.

47
00:04:06,336 --> 00:04:08,044
Five of them said enhanced.

48
00:04:09,813 --> 00:04:15,865
Beneath the table, in the margin, in the same small
neat hand, Evelyn had written a single

49
00:04:15,885 --> 00:04:18,631
note to herself, and underlined it twice.

50
00:04:19,199 --> 00:04:23,639
Tolerance correlates with baseline
aggression. Select accordingly.

51
00:04:25,709 --> 00:04:31,132
She photographed six pages. Her hands were shaking
so badly that she had to take two of

52
00:04:31,152 --> 00:04:36,575
them twice. Then she closed the binder, and squared
it exactly where it had been on the

53
00:04:36,595 --> 00:04:41,893
bench, and walked to the lifts, and pressed the
button, and stood very still, and did

54
00:04:41,913 --> 00:04:44,333
not breathe until the doors had closed.

55
00:04:44,903 --> 00:04:50,541
Evelyn came back fourteen minutes later, and picked
up the binder, and did not notice

56
00:04:50,561 --> 00:04:51,607
anything at all.

57
00:04:53,675 --> 00:04:56,016
She did not sleep that night, or the next.

58
00:04:56,586 --> 00:05:01,885
She thought about her mother in Lagos, whose
operation Umbrella's salary had paid for.

59
00:05:01,905 --> 00:05:07,265
She thought about her niece in Manchester, in the
second year of a medical degree Nadia

60
00:05:07,285 --> 00:05:12,584
was funding. She thought about Arthur Kowalczyk on
his motorcycle, and about the three

61
00:05:12,604 --> 00:05:18,026
hundred people now in the Aeterna trials whose lives
were getting better every week, and

62
00:05:18,046 --> 00:05:23,406
about what would happen to all of them if Umbrella
Life Sciences became, overnight, the

63
00:05:23,426 --> 00:05:25,571
subject of a federal investigation.

64
00:05:26,141 --> 00:05:31,742
And she thought about nineteen monkeys, and a note
in a margin. Select accordingly.

65
00:05:33,312 --> 00:05:38,656
At five in the morning, on the third night, she
opened her laptop and began to write.

66
00:05:40,725 --> 00:05:42,469
The Director knew by Monday.

67
00:05:43,040 --> 00:05:48,983
He knew it the way he knew most things now — across
a table, in the first ten seconds

68
00:05:49,003 --> 00:05:54,946
of a meeting. Nadia Okafor sat in her usual place,
and said good morning in her usual

69
00:05:54,966 --> 00:05:59,858
voice, and was, to every other person at the
table, perfectly herself.

70
00:06:00,427 --> 00:06:06,274
To the Cold-Read she was a flare in the dark. Fear,
bright and white. Grief — a great

71
00:06:06,294 --> 00:06:12,416
deal of grief, which surprised him. And beneath it,
like bedrock, something the Cold-Read

72
00:06:12,436 --> 00:06:18,766
rarely showed him in anyone: a decision that had
already been made, and would not be unmade.

73
00:06:20,456 --> 00:06:22,349
She is going to betray you.

74
00:06:22,870 --> 00:06:25,367
She's going to leave. It isn't
the same thing.

75
00:06:25,887 --> 00:06:30,304
She has seen something. She will take it
to your enemies. Stop her.

76
00:06:30,824 --> 00:06:31,380
How?

77
00:06:31,899 --> 00:06:33,245
You know how.

78
00:06:33,766 --> 00:06:38,055
Say it, then. Say it out loud, since you've
got nothing but time in there.

79
00:06:39,574 --> 00:06:47,593
She has a mother in Lagos. And a niece in Manchester.
People with families can be persuaded

80
00:06:47,613 --> 00:06:49,979
to remember their families.

81
00:06:51,999 --> 00:06:58,538
The Director did not answer that for the rest of the
meeting. He discussed the assay launch,

82
00:06:58,558 --> 00:07:04,741
and the Aeterna phase three enrolment, and a new
facility in Singapore. He did not look

83
00:07:04,761 --> 00:07:06,239
at Nadia Okafor once.

84
00:07:06,810 --> 00:07:12,715
But in the quiet of one skull, for forty minutes, a
very old argument was taking place,

85
00:07:12,735 --> 00:07:17,075
and it was not the argument either of
them had expected to have.

86
00:07:18,964 --> 00:07:21,118
We are not going to threaten her family.

87
00:07:21,637 --> 00:07:27,219
Why not? You are not squeamish. I have been inside
your head for two years. You are the

88
00:07:27,239 --> 00:07:32,241
least squeamish man I have ever met, and I have
met — I have met a great many.

89
00:07:32,762 --> 00:07:38,325
It isn't squeamishness. It's arithmetic. If I
threaten her family, she becomes a martyr

90
00:07:38,345 --> 00:07:44,486
with a motive. If I let her go, she becomes a disgruntled
former employee with some photographs.

91
00:07:44,506 --> 00:07:48,530
One of those makes the evening news. The
other makes page nine.

92
00:07:49,050 --> 00:07:50,609
And the photographs?

93
00:07:51,129 --> 00:07:56,613
Show a large grey sculpture and a table of numbers
in a binder. The FBI will come. The

94
00:07:56,633 --> 00:08:02,053
CDC will come. They'll come to a clean company with
a spotless inspection history and

95
00:08:02,073 --> 00:08:07,493
a drug that makes old men walk. And they'll find
nothing, because what they'd need to

96
00:08:07,513 --> 00:08:09,733
find isn't anywhere they can reach.

97
00:08:11,252 --> 00:08:13,016
You want them to come.

98
00:08:13,536 --> 00:08:18,817
I want them to come now. While I choose the ground.
A controlled burn, Adolf. You set

99
00:08:18,837 --> 00:08:22,870
a small fire on purpose, so there's
nothing left for the big one.

100
00:08:24,389 --> 00:08:26,461
And if you are wrong?

101
00:08:26,980 --> 00:08:32,375
Then I'll have learned something about this world.
That's worth more than one virologist.

102
00:08:34,395 --> 00:08:39,687
She came to resign in person. She could have sent
an email. She could have walked out

103
00:08:39,707 --> 00:08:45,248
on a Friday night and never come back. She came to
his office instead, at half past four,

104
00:08:45,268 --> 00:08:50,684
and knocked, because she was a person who knocked,
and she handed him a single sheet of

105
00:08:50,704 --> 00:08:51,058
paper.

106
00:08:51,749 --> 00:08:57,166
I'm leaving, Albert. Effective today. I'm sorry for the
short notice. I think you'll understand

107
00:08:57,186 --> 00:08:57,394
why.

108
00:08:57,913 --> 00:08:58,619
I do.

109
00:09:00,139 --> 00:09:05,599
She had expected him to ask. She had rehearsed the
conversation in the car. She had rehearsed

110
00:09:05,619 --> 00:09:10,903
what she would say when he asked what she had seen,
and what she would say when he offered

111
00:09:10,923 --> 00:09:16,031
her money, and — though she had not let herself
think about it clearly — what she would

112
00:09:16,051 --> 00:09:17,799
say if he did something worse.

113
00:09:18,370 --> 00:09:23,405
He did none of it. He simply looked at the letter,
and then at her, and then took off

114
00:09:23,425 --> 00:09:25,783
his sunglasses and set them on the desk.

115
00:09:26,352 --> 00:09:32,321
His eyes were ordinary. Pale, and grey, and very
tired, in a way she had never seen before.

116
00:09:32,341 --> 00:09:38,178
She would think about that for years. She would
wonder whether he had done it on purpose.

117
00:09:38,869 --> 00:09:44,344
You're going to go to the CDC. And the FDA.
And they'll pass it to the FBI.

118
00:09:44,864 --> 00:09:45,459
Yes.

119
00:09:45,979 --> 00:09:50,837
They'll come. They'll come here, to this building,
with warrants and badges, and they'll

120
00:09:50,857 --> 00:09:55,827
find a clean company with a spotless record, and a
drug that makes old men walk, and three

121
00:09:55,847 --> 00:10:00,761
hundred patients who'll tell them it saved their
lives. And you'll spend the rest of your

122
00:10:00,781 --> 00:10:03,644
career being the woman who was
wrong about Umbrella.

123
00:10:05,364 --> 00:10:06,378
Then I'll be wrong.

124
00:10:08,398 --> 00:10:14,008
He looked at her for a long time. The Cold-Read
showed him exactly what she felt, and

125
00:10:14,028 --> 00:10:19,970
it was the one thing, in two years, that he had not
been able to make anyone in this world

126
00:10:19,990 --> 00:10:25,401
feel toward him, not with money, not with fear,
not with a speech in a break room.

127
00:10:25,971 --> 00:10:29,038
She was not afraid of him. She
was sorry for him.

128
00:10:30,729 --> 00:10:31,818
Goodbye, Nadia.

129
00:10:32,337 --> 00:10:33,341
Goodbye, Albert.

130
00:10:34,861 --> 00:10:40,245
She took her phone with her. He did not ask for it.
She walked out of the office, and

131
00:10:40,265 --> 00:10:45,776
down the corridor, and past Hale, who stood by the
lifts with his hands folded in front

132
00:10:45,796 --> 00:10:50,671
of him and watched her go, and did not move,
because he had not been told to.

133
00:10:52,740 --> 00:10:54,908
Dana Whitfield caught her in the lobby.

134
00:10:55,479 --> 00:11:01,617
Nobody had told Dana. Nobody needed to. She had worked
with Nadia Okafor for twelve years,

135
00:11:01,637 --> 00:11:07,569
and she saw her face, and the box of things from her
desk, and she came out from behind

136
00:11:07,589 --> 00:11:12,290
the reception desk and hugged her, hard, in
front of the living wall.

137
00:11:12,980 --> 00:11:13,892
Be careful.

138
00:11:14,412 --> 00:11:15,215
You too.

139
00:11:16,735 --> 00:11:22,369
Dana let her go. She stood in the lobby for a long
time after the doors had closed, looking

140
00:11:22,389 --> 00:11:27,836
out at the rain. She did not know what Nadia had
seen. She did not want to know. But she

141
00:11:27,856 --> 00:11:33,489
had noticed, for the first time that week, that she
had started saying Be careful to people

142
00:11:33,509 --> 00:11:37,216
who were leaving the building, instead
of Have a good night.

143
00:11:37,787 --> 00:11:41,900
She did not know when that had started. She
thought it had been a while.

144
00:11:43,970 --> 00:11:46,951
Nadia Okafor walked out into the rain.

145
00:11:47,520 --> 00:11:53,309
She did not open an umbrella. She had one in her
bag. She would say afterwards that she

146
00:11:53,329 --> 00:11:59,318
simply did not want to, that night, and she would not
be able to explain why, and everyone

147
00:11:59,338 --> 00:12:00,453
would understand.

148
00:12:03,024 --> 00:12:08,845
The complaint reached the Centers for Disease Control
and Prevention in Atlanta nineteen

149
00:12:08,865 --> 00:12:14,753
days later, through a portal most people did not know
existed, and was assigned — because

150
00:12:14,773 --> 00:12:20,528
she was the most junior officer on the relevant
desk, and because nobody else wanted it

151
00:12:20,548 --> 00:12:26,171
— to a thirty-nine-year-old epidemic intelligence
officer named Doctor Samira Haddad.

152
00:12:26,740 --> 00:12:31,745
She read it at her desk at six in the evening,
with a sandwich she forgot to eat.

153
00:12:32,316 --> 00:12:37,731
She looked at the photograph of the grey figure
first, and she laughed out loud, once,

154
00:12:37,751 --> 00:12:39,754
and said to nobody: oh, come on.

155
00:12:40,323 --> 00:12:46,747
Then she looked at the table. Twenty-four macaques.
A compound with no registered name.

156
00:12:46,767 --> 00:12:53,783
Nineteen deceased. Five enhanced. And a note in a
margin, in small neat handwriting, underlined

157
00:12:53,803 --> 00:12:54,228
twice.

158
00:12:54,798 --> 00:12:56,105
She stopped laughing.

159
00:12:57,674 --> 00:13:03,248
Samira Haddad had spent four years chasing outbreaks
in places where nobody else wanted

160
00:13:03,268 --> 00:13:09,228
to go. She had learned that most complaints are
nothing, and that the ones that are something

161
00:13:09,248 --> 00:13:14,822
rarely look like it at first, and that the most
dangerous words in her profession were:

162
00:13:14,842 --> 00:13:16,108
that can't be right.

163
00:13:16,677 --> 00:13:21,933
She read the complaint twice more. Then she picked
up the phone, and called a friend at

164
00:13:21,953 --> 00:13:24,540
the Food and Drug
Administration, and said:

165
00:13:25,230 --> 00:13:27,841
Hi, it's Sam. Listen. Who do you
know at the Bureau?
